sec.p:
To quantify the contribution of distractor orientations reports to the observed behavioral responses, we fit, for each participant, a three-component mixture model – comprising two von Mises distributions and a uniform distribution 29:


p(t^)=αf(t^;t,κt)+βf(t^;d,κd)+γ12π;α+β+γ=1
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle p(\hat{t})=\alpha f\left(\hat{t} ; t, \kappa_{t}\right)+\beta f\left(\hat{t} ; d, \kappa_{d}\right)+\gamma \frac{1}{2 \pi} ; \quad \alpha+\beta+\gamma=1$$\end{document}

Next, to evaluate the contribution of distractor orientation reports, we fit a simpler mixture model excluding the distractor von Mises component; we term this the “target alone” model:


p′(t^)=αf(t^;t,κt)+β12π;α+β=1
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle p^{\prime}(\hat{t})=\alpha f\left(\hat{t} ; t, \kappa_{t}\right)+\beta \frac{1}{2 \pi} ; \quad \alpha+\beta=1$$\end{document}

For ease of illustration, we start by describing the dynamical equation governing the firing rate r of the cued visual cortex attractor (VC,C) as a function of time t27,28.
(1)
τEdrEVC,Cdt=−rEVC,C+ϕ(WEEVC,CrEVC,C−WIEVC,CrIVC,C+IEVC,C+CEVC,C)+σEVC,Cξ(t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau_{E} \frac{d r_{E}^{V C, C}}{d t}=-r_{E}^{V C, C}+\phi\left(W_{E E}^{V C, C} r_{E}^{V C, C}-W_{I E}^{V C, C} r_{I}^{V C, C}+I_{E}^{V C, C}+C_{E}^{V C, C}\right)+\sigma_{E}^{V C, C} \xi(t) \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(2)
τIdrIVC,Cdt=−rIVC,C+ϕ(WEIVC,CrEVC,C−WIIVC,CrIVC,C+CIVC,C)+σIVC,Cξ(t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau_{I} \frac{d r_{I}^{V C, C}}{d t}=-r_{I}^{V C, C}+\phi\left(W_{E I}^{V C, C} r_{E}^{V C, C}-W_{I I}^{V C, C} r_{I}^{V C, C}+C_{I}^{V C, C}\right)+\sigma_{I}^{V C, C} \xi(t) \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Where, rEVC,C\begin{document}$r_{E}^{V C, C}$\end{document} and rIVC,C\begin{document}$r_{I}^{V C, C}$\end{document} denote the firing rate of the excitatory and inhibitory neurons of the visual cortex, cued attractor (VC,C) respectively. ΤE and τI are neuronal (integration) time constants for E and I neurons respectively, WEEVC,C,WIEVC,C,WEIVC,C,WIIVC,C\begin{document}$W_{E E}^{V C, C}, W_{I E}^{V C, C}, W_{E I}^{V C, C}, W_{I I}^{V C, C}$\end{document} are the connection strengths from E to E, I to E, E to I and I to I neurons respectively for the VC,C attractor. CEVC,C\begin{document}$C_{E}^{V C, C}$\end{document} and CIVC,C\begin{document}$C_{I}^{V C, C}$\end{document} are the constant input bias currents to E and I neurons respectively of VC,C attractor. Ξ(t) represents Gaussian white noise with unit variance, scaled by parameters σEVC,C\begin{document}$\sigma_{E}^{V C, C}$\end{document} and σIVC,C\begin{document}$\sigma_{I}^{V C, C}$\end{document} for E and I neurons of VC,C attractor respectively. IEVC,C\begin{document}$I_{\mathrm{E}}^{V C, C}$\end{document} represents the input from other sources (explained in equations 3 and 4, see next). ϕ represents the function that maps the input current to the output firing rate adopted from Wimmer et al., 201427


ϕ(r)={0,∀r<0r2,∀0<r<1sqrt(4r−3),∀r≥1
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \phi(r)=\left\{\begin{array}{lr}0, & \forall r<0 \\ r^{2}, & \forall 0<r<1 \\ \operatorname{sqrt}(4 r-3), & \forall r \geq 1\end{array}\right.$$\end{document}

Inputs to the cued visual cortex attractor (IEVC,C\begin{document}$I_{\mathrm{E}}^{V C, C}$\end{document}, equation 1) comprised 3 contributions:
(3)
IEVC,C=UVC,C+WEHC,C→VC,CrEHC,C+WIVC,U→VC,CrEVC,U \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{\mathrm{E}}^{V C, C}=U^{V C, C}+W_{E}^{H C, C \rightarrow V C, C} r_{E}^{H C, C}+W_{I}^{V C, U \rightarrow V C, C} r_{E}^{V C, U} \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Inputs to the cued higher cortex attractor (IEVC,C\begin{document}$I_{\mathrm{E}}^{V C, C}$\end{document}, equation 1) comprised only one contribution corresponding to bottom-up connections from the VC,C attractor, also organized topographically (one-to-one):
(4)
IEHC,C=WEVC,C→HC,CrEVC,C \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} I_{E}^{H C, C}=W_{E}^{V C, C \rightarrow H C, C} r_{E}^{V C, C} \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

500 ms after the trial onset the memorandum was presented for a duration of 500 ms. The visual cortex (VC) attractor receives sensory input during this period (through UVC,C and UVC,Uterms in equation 3). We modeled this by changing the spatial profile of the input current to the excitatory neurons of the VC attractor according to:
(5)
UiVC,C=AM∗exp⁡(κMcos⁡(θi−ΘMC)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} U_{i}^{V C, C}=A_{M} * \exp \left(\kappa_{M} \cos \left(\theta_{i}-\Theta_{M}^{C}\right)\right) \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The distractor was presented for a duration of 0.1 seconds, at a random time (distributed exponentially) between 1200 ms and 1950 ms after the memory array onset, mimicking the behavioral paradigm. The distractor was presented either in the cued or the uncued hemifield with equal probability and was encoded by the visual cortex (VC) attractor representing the corresponding visual hemifield. Distractor presentation was modeled by adjusting the spatial profile of the input current to the excitatory neurons of the VC attractor as follows:
(6)
UiVC,C=AD∗exp⁡(κDcos⁡(θi−ΘD)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} U_{i}^{V C, C}=A_{D} * \exp \left(\kappa_{D} \cos \left(\theta_{i}-\Theta_{D}\right)\right) \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

We ran a total of 10,000 simulations with a different random seed each time; all results represent an average of these simulations. During each simulation, we randomly sampled the memory array (cued and uncued) and distractor orientation from a circular uniform random distribution. The “neural” orientation maintained by the network was decoded using the population vector representation in the visual cortex attractor given by:
(7)
θ^=arg⁡(∑j=1NrE,jVC,Ceiθj) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{\theta}=\arg \left(\sum_{j=1}^{N} r_{E, j}^{V C, C} \mathrm{e}^{i \theta_{j}}\right) \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
